  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 354 AM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nBroad cyclonic flow is noted over the northwest portion of the CONUS \nwith a couple of shortwaves in Canada helping to contribute to that \npattern, while a weaker shortwave can be seen pushing southeast of \nthe local area, leaving more zonal flow aloft for today. Surface \nhigh pressure is influencing eastern KS, and clear skies and light \nwinds have allowed us to reach crossover temperatures from yesterday \nwith fog observed in parts of east central KS. So far fog has been \non the patchy side, though visibility could quickly drop to a half \nmile or less in spots, particularly low-lying places and those spots \nthat saw more rain recently. HREF probs for half mile or less show a \ndecent signal for dense fog for a few hours around sunrise in \neastern KS. Will continue to monitor obs and webcams to determine \nthe need for a Dense Fog Advisory this morning if those lower \nvisibilities indeed become more widespread. Forecast soundings show \nfog mixing out around 14z, at which point visibility should improve.\n\nAs mentioned previously, the main challenge with the forecast \nbecomes timing and location of the greatest rain chances. CAMs are \nnot excited about developing storms along the frontal boundary in \nsouthern Nebraska this evening, the 06z runs even less so than the \n00z. Even if something isolated were to develop as 00z runs \nindicate, the instability axis is fairly narrow and mainly west of \nthe area with a less favorable environment further east in terms of \nboth instability and shear. Would think storms would have a harder \ntime maintaining themselves into our area with that setup, plus \nthere isn't a lot of forcing to support maintenance. The frontal \nboundary sags south through the day Saturday and would provide a \nbetter source of lift for thunderstorms. Assuming we don't have much \nin the way of rain or cloud cover into Saturday morning, we would \nalso have better instability with MLCAPE increasing to 3000-4000 \nJ/kg by late afternoon alongside deep-layer shear around 40 kts. \nThis could support a few strong or severe storms with damaging wind \nand small hail being the main hazards into the evening. Pwat around \n2\" could lead to brief heavy downpours as well, though widespread \nflooding is not anticipated with storms looking to be more \nscattered. It is worth noting that recent models seem to be placing \nthe front further south, which would favor southern portions of the \narea during this time frame.\n\nAs a deep upper low comes onshore of the Pacific Northwest and the \nupper ridge strengthens slightly just to our south, the sfc front \nlooks to lift back north as a warm front Sunday into Monday. This \nleads to southwesterly flow developing aloft and warming \ntemperatures. While some rain chances linger into Sunday, a general \ndrying and clearing trend is expected from south to north through \nthe day. Most of Monday looks dry until the evening hours when \nstorms develop along the front, which at this time looks to be \nplaced from southeastern NE through north central KS with the sfc \nlow located in southwest KS into the OK/TX panhandles. With the \naforementioned upper ridge strengthening over the southeastern US by \nTuesday, this slows the southward movement of the sfc front which \nmay stall over the region on Tuesday. This keeps likely PoPs over \nmuch of the area during the day. Tuesday onward is also when we \nbegin to see large spreads in temperatures, which will be tied to \nthe exact location of the front as well as clouds and rain. The \nfront may again move back north on Wednesday depending on the \nstrength of the upper ridge. Uncertainty in the evolution of that \npattern leads to lower confidence in the later part of the forecast \nperiod in terms of precipitation and temperatures, with at least \nsome rain chances persisting through mid-week.",
